1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,390
Maya: He was convicted as a spy. He was innocent.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:12,650
Alex: Meet Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish officer in the French army. In eighteen-ninety-four, he was wrongly convicted of treason and sent to prison.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:23,130
Maya: The case involved secret evidence, forged documents, and anti-Jewish prejudice. His family and supporters kept challenging it. Later evidence pointed to a different officer.

4
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:35,590
Alex: But correcting the case took years. Dreyfus was finally cleared in nineteen-oh-six. This wasn’t just an argument in a newspaper. A real person lost years of freedom.

5
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:44,600
Maya: The lesson is not that every accusation is false. It’s that someone’s Jewish identity is not proof that they’re disloyal. You still need reliable evidence.

6
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:54,170
Alex: If a case depends on prejudice instead of facts, looking confident doesn’t fix it. Neither does a crowd.

7
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,970
Maya: A label is not evidence. Ask what actually happened.

